**Current support and accepted design.** Template-head directives are implemented. General source modifiers, including `$config`, remain accepted design queued for implementation. Entry selection instead uses the planned fixed root filename `@entry.moth`. `config.moth` retains its named `project` and builder-specific records. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

A directive modifies an existing construct. Template-head directives modify their template and source directives modify the following eligible construct.

### Two attachment locations

The directive's compiler-defined contract determines its eligible target. Position cannot turn a source modifier into a template directive or a module-wide marker.

[table:
    [th:
        [: Location]
        [: Target]
        [: Example]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Inside a template head]
        [: The containing template]
        [: `[$slot title]` or `[$md: text]`]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Before a source declaration or statement]
        [: Exactly the next eligible construct]
        [: `$config` followed by `api_url #String`]
    ]
]

A source modifier has its own line and must attach to the next eligible construct in the same scope. Contiguous comment-only lines may intervene, but **any blank physical line breaks attachment**, including whitespace-only lines and blank lines between comments. A source modifier cannot cross an unrelated item, ending scope or file boundary. Compatible modifiers may stack without blank lines; duplicates and incompatible combinations are errors. A modifier at the very beginning of a file still attaches to its following declaration.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Accepted source modifier syntax:
    $config
    api_url #String = "https://example.com"
]

The source example describes accepted syntax, not current Alpha compiler support. No module-root directive category exists. The proposed fixed-root-roles module structure instead uses `@mod.moth` for the normal API facade, `@package.moth` for the scoped or external package facade and `@entry.moth` for executable entry work. `config.moth` remains separate. The compiler and builder select entries from the role facts, not from directives or authored runtime activity.
### Operands and ownership

The compiler defines directive names, valid target kinds, phases and operand contracts. A directive has at most one optional compile-time operand:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    $name
    $name value
    $name key = value
]

A named operand is one key/value pair, not a parameter list. It has no authored type annotation. Values reuse existing compile-time expression rules where the directive permits a value. A directive may require a specific literal shape, phase or value type. Mutable, runtime or side-effectful operands are invalid. Call-style `$name(...)` and arbitrary `$name: ... ;` source blocks are outside this contract. Ordinary Moth calls such as `f()` are unaffected.

Template heads keep their own delimiters and static slot labels. `$slot title`, `$slot 1`, `$slot title = "Fallback"`, `$in title` and `$fresh` are examples. Current `$before = value` and `$after = value` wrappers migrate to `$before value` and `$after value` under the general plan. `$code("moth")` migrates to a single operand. These are syntax migrations, not new template behaviours. See @../templates/#template-directives (Template directives).

Directives are compiler-owned modifiers, not attributes available to arbitrary source code, macros, user-defined annotations or new independent control-flow constructs. Builders may supply configuration and destination schemas, but cannot register new directive names or semantic callbacks once the general plan lands. Directive names do not reserve matching ordinary source identifiers.

### Examples and contexts

- `$config` marks one explicitly typed top-level compile-time `#` constant as an input contract. Its type and fallback are written on the declaration itself. It is valid in selected bootstrap configuration and eligible selected source modules. `#Config of T` remains the operational spelling until this migration.
- There is no entry-purpose directive. `@entry.moth` identifies an eligible executable entry under the fixed-root-roles design. `$page` is not an alias.
- `$test`, `$export`, `$layout` and computation constraints are separate planned designs. Their exact targets and semantics are not implemented by this reference.
- `$async` and `$checked` remain undecided proposals. Their earlier block spellings do not establish a general-purpose directive block grammar.
- Project and active-builder settings remain ordinary named compile-time records inside `config.moth`. There are no accepted `$project`, `$builder` or `$html_builder` directives.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Operational named configuration records:
    project #= (
        name = "my_site",
    )

    html #= ()
]

See @../project-structure/#project-config (Project configuration), @../project-structure/#build-inputs (Build configuration values) and @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).

### Diagnostics and recovery

The compiler recognises a known directive name **before** validating context, so a known directive outside its allowed location receives a placement diagnostic rather than an unknown-name or generic invalid-character diagnostic. Distinguish unknown names, known but unavailable/deferred names, invalid target kinds, invalid operand shapes and incompatible combinations.

Diagnostics identify the directive's authored span, the target when present and the nearest useful fix:

- `$config` separated from its declaration by an empty line, unrelated item or scope boundary: place the target directly after the modifier. A wrong target such as a function or local value identifies the allowed declaration kind.
- `$md` in ordinary source or `$config` inside a template head: use the valid directive context. A `$slot` in a malformed head receives its existing structural diagnostics.
- Unknown spellings and recognised but unimplemented directives have distinct diagnostics. Invalid directives never act as successful no-ops and recovery must not consume following unrelated source.

A declaration modifier appearing first in a file is still a declaration modifier. Directive matching never depends on capitalisation. CRLF and LF line endings must have identical attachment semantics.
